---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DetailPage from '../../components/DetailPage.astro';
import PdfViewer from '../../components/PdfViewer.astro';
import chargingProfiles from '../../assets/coursework/charging-profiles.png';
import dailyEvDemand from '../../assets/coursework/daily-ev-demand.png';
import modelOutput from '../../assets/coursework/model-output.png';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Capstone: Ottawa's 2050 Energy System"
  description="SESP capstone: projecting Ottawa's 2050 EV load and optimizing the city's future energy mix."
>
  <DetailPage
    backHref="/coursework/"
    backLabel="Coursework"
    title="Capstone: Ottawa's 2050 Energy System"
    meta="Sustainable Energy Systems Portfolio, Carleton University · 2023 – 2024"
    tags={['Integration team lead', 'MATLAB', 'MILP', 'EV demand', 'Sensitivity analysis']}
  >
    <p>
      The Sustainable Energy Systems Portfolio (SESP) is a year-long, multidisciplinary capstone.
      Our goal was to work out how Ottawa could meet its 2050 electricity needs using the
      technologies our sub-teams were developing: a buoyant airborne wind turbine, solar, small
      modular reactors, a hybrid lithium-ion/flywheel storage system, and building efficiency
      upgrades. As integration team lead for 25+ students, I brought those pieces together into
      one model of the city's energy system.
    </p>

    <h2>Fall: projecting Ottawa's 2050 EV load</h2>
    <p>
      I built a method to project the city's hourly electric vehicle demand in 2050 from empirical
      data. It combines residential, workplace, and weekend charging profiles; a projection of
      about 412,000 EVs on the road; a temperature model that raises energy use by up to 50% in
      cold weather; and commute distances calculated from Ottawa's origin-destination survey with
      QGIS and the Google Distance Matrix API.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <Image src={chargingProfiles} alt="Line chart of hourly EV charging by profile: workplace charging peaks mid-morning, weekend in the late afternoon, and residential in the evening." />
      <figcaption>Charging profiles: share of daily charging by hour for each profile.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <Image src={dailyEvDemand} alt="Daily EV energy demand across 2050, highest in winter at around 5,500 to 6,000 MWh per day and lowest in summer at around 4,000 MWh." />
      <figcaption>Projected daily EV energy demand for 2050. Demand peaks in winter, when cold weather raises each vehicle's energy use.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Winter: a least-cost optimization model</h2>
    <p>
      I then built a mixed-integer linear programming model in MATLAB that finds the cheapest mix
      of generation and storage that meets Ottawa's load every hour. It covers each sub-team's
      technology, using the constraints they supplied, plus imports from the Ontario grid. I used
      parallel processing to run many scenarios at once, varying the allowed share of imports,
      carbon prices, and three building-retrofit load scenarios.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <Image src={modelOutput} alt="Stacked area chart of hourly generation by source over four representative weeks: nuclear provides the base, with wind, solar, and imports filling the peaks." />
      <figcaption>Model output: hourly generation by source (heat pumps only, 50% imports allowed).</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The results showed the cost of full decarbonization. In the limited-retrofits scenario,
      cutting imports from 100% to zero raised the annual system cost from $14.2B to $22.4B and
      required about 27 GW of solar, with heavy curtailment. With heat pumps alone and no imports,
      the sub-teams' technologies could not meet demand at all.
    </p>

    <h2>Reports</h2>
    <h3>Final report</h3>
    <PdfViewer src="/documents/sesp-final-report.pdf" title="SESP Integration Team Final Report" pages={17} />

    <h3>Fall interim report</h3>
    <PdfViewer src="/documents/sesp-interim-report.pdf" title="SESP Fall Interim Report" pages={16} />
  </DetailPage>
</BaseLayout>
